        "A_Q1": { "score": 5, "rationale": "Best-case world for a content-first strategy. Growing at-home market with no tech giant pressure means premium content commands premium pricing. Community features create switching costs." },
        "A_Q2": { "score": 3, "rationale": "Content is strong but big tech can outspend on production. Apple could sign exclusive instructors. The community moat helps but may not be enough against subsidized competition." },
        "A_Q3": { "score": 1, "rationale": "Worst case. Shrinking at-home demand plus big tech competition. Content investment has high fixed costs that don't scale down. Subscriber losses make the unit economics collapse." },
        "A_Q4": { "score": 3, "rationale": "Smaller but loyal audience values premium content. Without big tech pressure, Apex can sustain pricing. But the ceiling is low — you're investing $40M for a niche." },
        "B_Q1": { "score": 3, "rationale": "Corporate wellness works in any fitness modality, but the at-home tailwind helps. Employers see value in subsidizing home fitness. However, this diverts focus from the consumer opportunity in a golden-age market." },
